meta {
  name: Get Invite (Public)
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/auth/invite/{{inviteToken}}
  auth: none
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("greets the invitee with agency and role", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.email).to.be.a("string");
    expect(body.agencyName).to.be.a("string");
    expect(body.roleNames).to.be.an("array").that.is.not.empty;
  });

  test("an employee invite has no agency setup waiting behind it", function () {
    // True only for the owner of a freshly onboarded agency (PAC-69).
    expect(res.getBody().agencySetupPending).to.equal(false);
  });

  test("discloses nothing beyond the documented fields", function () {
    // The non-disclosure contract. This is an unauthenticated endpoint, so a new
    // field appearing here is a leak to anyone holding a forwarded link — this
    // test is meant to fail when one is added without thought.
    //
    // The three PAC-69 added were held to the same rule: `firstName` and
    // `lastName` are what the inviter typed and already appear in the greeting
    // of the email the link came from, and `agencySetupPending` is a fact about
    // the *agency's* setup state rather than about the person.
    expect(Object.keys(res.getBody()).sort()).to.eql([
      "agencyName",
      "agencySetupPending",
      "email",
      "expiresAt",
      "firstName",
      "lastName",
      "roleNames",
    ]);
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /auth/invite/:token — public invite preview

  Public (`@Public()`, `auth: none`). Lets the accept-invite page greet the
  invitee — "You've been invited to *Smith Family Agency* as *Producer*" — before
  they have any credentials.

  **Response `200`**
  ```json
  {
    "email": "pending@example.com",
    "agencyName": "Smith Family Agency",
    "roleNames": ["Producer"],
    "expiresAt": "2026-08-24T09:00:00.000Z",
    "firstName": "Pending",
    "lastName": "Invitee",
    "agencySetupPending": false
  }
  ```

  Every field is something the holder of the emailed link already knows. No user
  id, nothing that would turn a token into a directory lookup.

  The last three arrived with PAC-69 and were held to the same rule.
  `firstName` / `lastName` are what the inviter typed and already appear in the
  greeting of the email the link came from — they let the onboarding wizard show
  the names prefilled rather than asking the invitee to retype what is already on
  the record. `agencySetupPending` is a fact about the **agency's** setup state,
  not about the person: it is true only for the owner of a freshly onboarded
  agency, and is here so the wizard can size its step counter ("Step 1 of 5")
  before any session exists.

  **Errors**
  - `410 Gone` — the token is valid but expired. The page tells the invitee to
    ask for a resend, which is actionable.
  - `404 Not Found` — unknown token, or one already used. Generic on purpose.

  ## Folder placement
  This is an `auth/*` route but lives under **Users**, not **Auth**, for the same
  ordering reason activity logging lives under **Leads**: the CLI walks folders
  alphabetically, so an `Auth` copy would run before any invite existed and have
  no token to send. It sits here, immediately after the invite that mints it.
}
